{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1692361792/10/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"80214e03\" lines=\"48-64\">\n<description>\nExtract the color of each noisy block in a row/column lattice and mirror the coarse grid horizontally.\n\nInput structure:\n- A background surrounds a rectangular lattice of monochrome foreground blocks.\n- Consecutive rows in a lattice band share the same set of visible foreground colors.\n- Consecutive columns in a lattice band share the same set of visible foreground colors.\n- Each row-band/column-band intersection has one foreground color, with possible background holes.\n\nRule:\n- Group non-empty rows and columns into consecutive bands by their foreground color sets.\n- Replace each lattice cell by the single foreground color inside that row/column-band intersection.\n- Return the resulting coarse color grid with the column order reversed.\n\nKey insight:\n- Whole-row and whole-column color sets reveal the hidden lattice even when neighboring cells share a color or contain holes.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"aaecdb9a\" lines=\"10-26\">\n<description>\nConvert scattered foreground objects into bottom-aligned count bars in a fixed five-color order.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background color and foreground objects in the task\npalette: grey, red, teal, maroon, and pink. Objects are monochromatic\n8-connected components; some palette colors may be absent.\n\nRule\nFor each palette color in order grey, red, teal, maroon, pink, count its\n8-connected objects. Create a background grid with five columns and height\nequal to the largest count, then fill each column upward from the bottom\nwith its palette color for that count.\n\nKey insight\nThe output does not preserve object shapes or positions; it is a histogram\nof per-color 8-connected object counts, using the learned palette order.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"6e453dd6\" lines=\"39-52\">\n<description>\nSlide black shapes rightward to a grey separator, then shoot red rays from enclosed holes.\n\nInput structure:\n- A vertical grey (5) separator column divides the grid into left and right halves.\n- Black shapes sit on the left side against a background color (bg).\n\nRule:\n- Translate each black connected component rightward until its right edge is flush with the separator.\n- After sliding, find enclosed bg regions (holes) within the black shapes that touch the column just left of the separator.\n- For each row spanned by such a hole, emit a red ray across the entire right half of the grid.\n\nKey insight: Only holes adjacent to the separator trigger rays — the sliding creates enclosures that act as \"nozzles\" projecting rightward.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9344f635\" lines=\"15-30\">\n<description>\nExtend each colored domino marker into a full row or column bar, with horizontal bars covering vertical bars at crossings.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Every foreground object is a 4-connected same-color domino of exactly two pixels.\n- A horizontal domino marks a row to paint; a vertical domino marks a column to paint.\n\nRule:\n- Paint each marked column entirely with the color of its vertical domino marker.\n- Paint each marked row entirely with the color of its horizontal domino marker.\n- Rows are painted after columns, so row bars overwrite column bars at intersections.\n\nKey insight:\n- The two-pixel orientation is the instruction: horizontal markers emit row bars, vertical markers emit column bars, and the only layering cue is row-over-column.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"494ef9d7\" lines=\"16-30\">\n<description>\nSlide each blue/teal or yellow/orange row pair together while preserving its left-to-right order.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a black background.\n- Each nonempty row contains exactly two distinct foreground pixels.\n- Blue/teal and yellow/orange row pairs are the moving pairs; other color pairs are distractors.\n\nRule\n- For each moving pair, keep the left pixel fixed and move the right pixel left until it touches the left pixel.\n- Clear the moved pixel's original location, and leave non-moving rows unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- Only same-hue-family pixel pairs attract horizontally; separated unrelated colors do not compact.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e729b7be\" lines=\"8-20\">\n<description>\nComplete the scene by adding the point-reflected copy of the colored object across the central marker.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has odd dimensions, a dominant background, and a center-row marker color appearing only at the left edge, center, and right edge.\n- A single divider color fills the center column except for the center marker.\n- All remaining foreground pixels form the object to copy, entirely on the left side of the divider.\nRule\n- Keep the input unchanged.\n- For every object pixel, paint the pixel rotated 180 degrees around the center marker with the same color.\nKey insight\n- The center marker is the rotation center; the yellow-looking divider is not mirrored, it only separates the source from the empty target side.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"dfadab01\" lines=\"79-93\">\n<description>\nExpand each colored control pixel into its corresponding fixed 4x4 glyph.\n\nInput structure:\n- Black background with single-pixel markers in colors 2, 3, 5, and/or 8.\n- Some inputs also show a sample 4x4 glyph in the output color, with a same-rule marker just below-right of the sample.\n\nRule:\n- Replace each marker color by its learned 4x4 glyph: 2->yellow frame, 3->blue ring, 5->pink diagonal blocks, 8->orange X.\n- Paint each glyph with its top-left corner at the marker pixel, clipping at the grid boundary.\n- Remove the sample glyphs and skip their below-right label markers.\n\nKey insight:\n- The colored pixels are control pixels for a fixed glyph alphabet; non-control sample glyphs are only legend entries, not preserved scene objects.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e3721c99\" lines=\"69-83\">\n<description>\nRecolor grey shapes by matching their hole count to a color legend.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color; grey (5) sprites scattered across the grid.\n- Colored legend shapes (non-bg, non-grey, at least 2×2, not touching the outer border) near grid edge,\n  each with a distinct number of enclosed holes.\n\nRule:\n- Each colored legend shape defines a mapping: hole count → color.\n- For every grey sprite, count its holes (enclosed bg regions not touching the sprite's bounding-box border).\n- Recolor the grey sprite to the matching legend color; erase grey distractors with unlisted hole counts.\n\nKey insight: Shape identity is encoded by topology (number of holes), not geometry or size.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"6e4f6532\" lines=\"26-41\">\n<description>\nDock train sprites onto station groups by matching coupler count, then orient via divider-color markers.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg; colored stripes (edge + internal) partition grid into rectangular regions.\n- Color 8 forms train bodies; color 9 marks both isolated stations and train-attached couplers.\n- Stripe-colored pixels on trains are orientation markers.\n\nRule:\n- Stations = 8-connected 9-clusters not adjacent to any 8. Trains = components containing 8.\n- Match train to station group where |couplers| == |station size|.\n- Rotate/flip train so marker pixels face the same-color boundary stripe of the target region.\n- Translate so couplers align exactly onto station cells; erase original position.\n\nKey insight: Coupler count selects the docking target; marker color encodes which boundary stripe to face, fixing the unique orientation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"58490d8a\" lines=\"9-21\">\n<description>\nCount colored objects and record tallies inside a legend box.\n\nInput structure:\n- A black rectangular legend box with key-color pixels in its leftmost non-black column.\n- Outside the box, distinct objects of each key color are scattered across the grid.\n\nRule:\n- For each key color, count separate same-colored objects outside the legend box.\n- Output the legend box repainted: each key row gets one dot per object, spaced every other cell rightward from the key pixel.\n\nKey insight: The legend is both the color index and the output canvas — object counts become dot sequences on the matching row.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"1ae2feb7\" lines=\"30-45\">\n<description>\nExtend row bars periodically across the opposite side of a separator column.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. A near-solid vertical separator divides the grid into two sides.\n- One side contains horizontal colored bars; the other side is blank target space.\n- Bars may be stacked by row and may sit at different distances from the separator.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the separator as the column whose non-bg cells are one color.\n- Treat the denser side as the bar side and collect each contiguous row run.\n- Process bars from nearest to farthest from the separator; for each bar, paint its\n  color every bar-length cells into the blank side without overwriting earlier paint.\n\nKey insight: Each bar's length is the period for its extension, and nearer bars claim target cells first.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"3cd86f4f\" lines=\"6-18\">\n<description>\nShear the rectangular input down-left onto a black canvas.\n\nInput structure\nA rectangular grid of colored pixels with no internal empty region to infer.\n\nRule\nCreate a black output with the same height and width W + H - 1, then copy\neach input row r unchanged starting at column H - 1 - r.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
